FIX Orchestra XSDs
------------------

upstream/v1.0/ holds the XML schemas of FIX Orchestra v1.0, the Technical
Standard, as FIX Protocol Limited (the FIX Trading Community) publishes them
in fix-orchestra-spec at the tag v1.0, commit e2b65db:
https://github.com/FIXTradingCommunity/fix-orchestra-spec. upstream/v1.1-rc3/
holds those of FIX Orchestra v1.1 Release Candidate 3, as FIX publishes them
in fix-orchestra at the tag v1.1-rc3, commit eb4bb9d:
https://github.com/FIXTradingCommunity/fix-orchestra. Each folder holds its
repository's LICENSE, and every file is unchanged.

  Copyright (c) 2016-2026 FIX Protocol Limited.

Each XSD names the Creative Commons Attribution-NoDerivatives 4.0
International Public License (CC BY-ND 4.0,
https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nd/4.0/), whose text
upstream/v1.0/LICENSE holds. The fix-orchestra code repository is under the
Apache License, Version 2.0, whose text upstream/v1.1-rc3/LICENSE holds. The
dc.xsd, dcterms.xsd and dcmitype.xsd files beside the XSDs are the Dublin
Core Metadata Initiative's, and xml.xsd is W3C's, as FIX copied them; they
let the XSDs validate offline.

CC BY-ND 4.0 lets anyone copy and share the XSDs unchanged, with credit and
a link to the licence (its section 2(a)(1)(A)), which is what upstream/
does. It does not let anyone share an adapted version. So the schema under
src/fix_orchestra/schema/, which scripts/orchestra_xsd_to_linkml.py writes,
takes from the XSDs only what makes a file valid: the names of their
elements, attributes and types, their structure and their value lists.
Those are facts of the format, which any validator of it must state alike.
Copyright protects the way FIX expressed the standard, not the format or the
methods it describes, and CC BY-ND 4.0 restricts nothing that copyright does
not (its section 8(a)).

The project nevertheless takes the stricter reading that European law
allows (Infopaq, C-5/08), under which even a short extract can be protected
when it shows its author's own choices. Every title and description in the
schema, the documentation and the generated files is this project's own,
written in scripts/orchestra_words.yaml from what each element does, and
not by rewording the XSDs' documentation or the Orchestra specification.
tests/test_own_words.py holds that no run of four words in them is a run of
the XSDs' documentation. Nothing was taken from fixtrading.org, whose terms
bar copying its content. The specification remains the authority on what
Orchestra means; this project's words describe the format and do not
replace it.

